    {
      "id": "decl-meaning",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -1400,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Meaning\n\n**What is this thing?**\n\nDeclaration is the artifact-level contract that gives a graph durable identity, intent, authority, dependencies, and exposed surfaces. It is a graph's semantic anchor and its implicit root — the entry point every consumer reaches first, without inventing a separate root node merely for plumbing.\n\nA graph without a Declaration is a fragment: content that hasn't yet been given an identity of its own."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "decl-invariants",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Invariants",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -860,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Invariants\n\n**What makes this thing a Declaration, specifically?**\n\n- Exactly one authoritative Declaration per graph (a fragment may have none; it never has two).\n- Owns `data.identity` — `graphId`, `name`, `version`, `description` — for the graph as a whole.\n- Owns `data.intent` — `kind` and `scope` — stating what kind of artifact this graph is.\n- Is reachable as the graph's implicit root port even when no other node explicitly claims that role.\n- Every other node that belongs to this graph shares its `graphId`."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "decl-contract",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Contract",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -320,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Contract\n\n**What data belongs to it?**\n\n```text\nidentity      { graphId, name, version, description }\nintent        { kind, scope }\ndeclaration   { kind, defaultSurfaceId, surfaces[] }\nauthority     { mutation{allowCreate,allowUpdate,allowDelete}, actors{humans,agents,tools} }\nsettings      { layout, autoSave }\ndocument      { url }\n```\n\nRequired at minimum: `identity.graphId`, `identity.name`, `identity.version`, `identity.description`, `intent.kind`, `intent.scope`, `declaration.kind`.\n\n**Removed, 2026-09-23: `declaration.targetMode`.** It declared whether this graph, when bridged to, presents itself as a single artifact (`'artifact'`) or a browsable bundle (`'library'`) — traced end to end through `deriveBridgeTargetResolutionFromGraph()` in `creationVocabulary.js`, it never actually changed that outcome: the branch that matters checks a literal `exportKind === 'class'`, and `exportKind` is decided first by an explicit request, then by a per-surface declared `exportKind`, and only as a last resort by whether the graph already self-identifies as a node-class another way. `targetMode` never won a tie in the paths that were checked. What actually makes a graph a \"kit\" is a fact already present and checked elsewhere: whether it has any `role:'export'` Bridge nodes at all (see the Bridge primitive's Behavior section). Removed the field, its editor dropdown, and its validation; the live derivation now reads `intent.kind` where it used to read the redundant flag.\n\n**Also removed, 2026-09-23: `settings.github` on the Declaration node itself.** It looked like part of the node's own data — the Contract table above used to list it right next to `layout`/`autoSave` — but the save path never actually puts it there. `Toolbar.js`'s save snapshot computes GitHub settings and hands them to `setManifestSettings()` in `manifestUtils.js`, and that function destructures `github` straight back out (`const { github: _ignoredGithub, ...authoritySettings } = settings`) and deletes it again if it somehow survives, every single save. GitHub repo/path/branch/installationId live only in the *file's* own top-level `settings.github`, one level up from any node's `data` — a different object entirely, needed by the commit flow specifically because it carries `branch` and `installationId`, which a bare ref can't encode. `document.url` (kept — still in the contract table above) already carries everything else a `github://` ref would: `repo` and `path` are both re-derivable from it via `parseGithubRef()`. Two defensive read paths (`interGraphEdge.js`, `reciprocalExpansionBridge.js`) still check `data.settings.github.path` as a fallback; that's checking for a value current code guarantees is never there."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "decl-ports",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Ports and Relationships",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 220,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 900,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Ports and Relationships\n\n**What semantic relationships does it expose?**\n\nA graph offers its presentation through **Surfaces** (see the Surface primitive). The Declaration has **one port for them, and that port accepts any number of Surfaces.** Each Surface is a chain of Views that ends in a Glyph.\n\n**Surfaces:**\n\n- `surface` (repeatable) — one edge per Surface, each pointing at the **first View** of that Surface's chain. The edge carries the Surface's id (`data.surfaceId`), which is its only identity: rename it there, delete the edge to remove the Surface, and if two edges share an id the first wins. The graph canvas is a Surface; a web view, a 3D view or a map view is another. They are all the same kind of thing.\n- **The chain.** View → View → … → **Glyph**. Views run richest to most compact, and each can claim its own continuous resolution threshold (`authoredViewResolution()`, already real and unbounded). A host walks the chain and takes the richest View that fits.\n- **Every Surface begins with a View.** There is no glyph-only Surface. A graph that should show as just a glyph draws an icon View that renders the glyph, and ends its chain with its ordinary Glyph.\n- **The Glyph ends the chain.** It is the Surface's compact symbolic identity and the floor of its ladder, so **every Surface carries its own glyph** instead of the whole graph sharing one. A web view and a 3D view can each have a mark that suits them.\n- The rungs formerly named `default-view`, `summary-view` and `icon-view` are now just positions in a chain, not separate relationships. `declaration.defaultSurfaceId` says which Surface a host uses when it doesn't ask for one.\n\nThis **supersedes** five relationships: `default-view`, `summary-view`, `icon-view`, `view-sequence` and the standalone `glyph`. They are not removed and not converted. A graph written with them still loads, and is read as **one** default Surface, never one Surface per relationship. Their ports stay on the Declaration so old edges still land, marked legacy.\n\nA Surface chooses what renders **inside** the graph's frame. It never resizes the frame, reflows neighbors or moves the camera.\n\n**Status.** In the app now: the `surface` port; new graphs are created with one Surface; the editor lets you drag from the `surface` handle to add a Surface and from a View's next-view handle to extend its chain, and lists each Surface with its chain and Glyph in the Declaration editor; the resolver reads Surfaces and reads legacy graphs as one default Surface; per-Surface glyph and resolution ladder; a host may say what it can render and is never handed a Surface it cannot; the validator checks a well-formed chain per Surface; and the documents an LLM or an agent reads describe Surfaces. **Not yet:** the edge classes (`surface`; `view-sequence` ending at a glyph) are changed locally but not published; the workspace and camera do not yet ask for a Surface other than the default; nothing renders a 3D View; the Declaration node's own card is still drawn by dedicated code, because the Declaration template is still a draft (`templates/declaration-template`).\n\n**Malformed chains.** Imported files do not pass through the editor's edge-class rules, so the resolver decides on its own, the same way every time, and every case still shows something: a cycle stops at the first repeated node; a branch follows the first outgoing edge; a Glyph ends the walk and anything after it is ignored; a chain that reaches something other than a View or Glyph stops there; a `surface` edge whose head is not a View is skipped; a chain with no Glyph falls back to the Declaration's own `data.glyph`; a `defaultSurfaceId` that names no Surface falls to the first. Each is reported as a warning, never an error. Still to check live: two Surfaces sharing one View.\n\n**The Declaration's own Views.** No relationship for them is needed. A Port, and the Declaration as the implicit root Port, connects to Views through the relationships it already has. The Declaration *node's* own presentation comes from a Declaration template graph (see Representations).\n\n**Embedding behavior:**\n\n- `default-handle` — points at a `graphReference` node. When this graph appears as a node inside a *parent* graph, this is what a new edge dragged from its collapsed handle actually targets — a fact about this graph's own embedding, not about how it presents itself.\n\n**Boundary:**\n\n- `boundary-begin` / `boundary-end` — the ends of a hand-authored chain of `membrane`-type control-point nodes, linked by `next` edges (`membraneChain.js`), forming a custom membrane *shape*. An alternative to the auto-computed rectangular extent (`graphExtent()`/`defaultMembraneBounds()`) for a graph that needs a boundary the rectangle can't express.\n\n**Entry points:**\n\n- `port` (repeatable) — an exposed, named entry point.\n- `landing-surface` — the single content node a portal lands on when entering this graph. At most one. Despite the name, it is not a Surface in the sense above.\n\nEach relationship **assigns** another node to a named role. The role lives on the Declaration's edge to that node, not as a property intrinsic to the node itself — the same node could be assigned a different role, or none, without changing anything about the node."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "decl-representations",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Representations",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": 760,
        "y": -400
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 420,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Representations\n\n**How is it represented?**\n\n**Like any other node.** A Declaration takes part in view resolution exactly as a class node or a template node does, with no privileged path of its own: a host resolves the richest View that fits the room and context it has — the same resolver, the same rule, for every node. Its Surfaces (see Ports and Relationships) describe the graph; its own card is resolved the same way, from its own default View.\n\n- **Its default is a View, not a special case.** The compact identity card (name, description, version) is simply the default View a Declaration comes with, the way any class or template comes with a default. When the author wires Views to it, those take over and resolve like any others. Nothing needs to be switched off to do this.\n- **Collapsed, it shows a Glyph**, the same as every other node, not a separate \"declaration mark\".\n- **Two presentations, two owners.** The Surfaces on the Declaration describe how the **graph** presents itself, embedded or opened. How the Declaration **node** presents itself on a canvas is a separate question, answered by its own default View through the same resolver. A Declaration is never rendered by the chains it exposes, so nothing can recurse into itself.\n\nCustom Declaration rendering was tried early on and set aside as too troublesome. That was when a Declaration needed a renderer of its own. It doesn't now: presentation is Surfaces, Views and Glyphs, independent of any one renderer, and the Declaration goes through them like everything else.\n\n**The card comes from a template.** A Declaration template graph will hold all of the Declaration's parts, and the Declaration resolves through that graph by the ordinary class binding, exactly as any class or template node does. There is no special relationship for it: a Port connects to Views, and the Declaration is the implicit root Port. An author who wants a different card uses a different template. Until that template is final the card is the built-in default View, drawn by dedicated code (`ManifestNode`) rather than through the resolver."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "decl-boundary",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -1400,
        "y": 260
      },
      "width": 480,
      "height": 340,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ac97492b-7402-428b-9bce-5c5e91c7f981"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive\n\n- Declaration does not render content. Rendering the graph's actual material is View and Content's job; Declaration only names which node fills each presentation role.\n- Declaration does not own node positions or layout. Where nodes sit on the canvas is unrelated to identity.\n- Declaration does not define *how* an exposed view looks — only *which* node is assigned to that role.\n- Declaration does not grant runtime capability beyond what `authority` explicitly states. Absence of a grant means the action is not permitted, not that it's merely undocumented.\n- Declaration is not a template. It describes one graph's own identity, not a reusable composition pattern for building other graphs (see Templates).\n- Declaration does not vouch for which custom node types are safe to use in the graph. That authority comes from Bridges, one per type, each pointing at a real class definition."
        }
      }
    },
    {
